Chapter: PrimClosure
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Tier 14: what a partial application is allowed to forget.

 A partial application is a heap object here -- a code pointer followed
 by the captured arguments, `(1 + num-captures) * 8` bytes, built by
 `emit-partial-application` in Emit/X86_64Compound.codex. The code
 pointer is an inline trampoline that shifts the arguments it is entered
 with into place behind the captures and jumps. There is no word in that
 object saying how many arguments are still owed, so the trampoline is
 correct only when it is entered with ALL of them at once. Enter it with
 fewer and it jumps anyway, with whatever happened to be in the argument
 registers.

 DevelopersRulebook.md:258-260 makes this the language's own model rather
 than an exotic case: a def emitted n-ary is "over-applied by applying
 the rest one at a time". One at a time is precisely what the
 representation cannot express. Finding 39 is the register, COMPILER-18
 the upstream row, PR 79 the ruling asked for -- whether the closure
 gains an arity word or under-application is refused where it is
 emitted.

 The rows here are RELATIONS, not values. Under-application usually
 yields the closure pointer itself, so the wrong answers are heap
 addresses in the 6291456 range -- unstable across any allocator change,
 and an address is the one thing a tier must never print. `47` against
 `not-47` says the same thing and keeps saying it.

 This tier is deliberately three lines. Adding shapes is not free: the
 probes established that a broken definition damages GOOD ones in the
 same chapter -- probe-closure-solo prints 47, and
 probe-closure-saturated faults on that same expression with only an
 unused `relay-once` added beside it. A tier that grows until it faults
 stops reporting anything at all, so the other shapes stay in their
 probes, where each one can die alone.

 `control-flat` calls `add3` at full arity. No closure is built, so it
 answers 47 on any arm that can add, and it runs FIRST because a fault
 halts the program and a control after the defect would never print.

 `control-pick` is a closure-returning definition with two return paths
 and no recursion. It reaches `add3 5` through the same annotation and
 the same three-argument application as the row below, so it is what
 makes `under-mutual` a statement about under-application rather than
 about closure-returning types in general. This chapter -- add3, pick,
 even-fn, odd-fn -- is probe-closure-mutual's, which is known to run
 both its lines to completion; that is why the control is `pick` and not
 something newly invented here.

 `even-fn 0` would be the tighter control, differing from the defect row
 only in the recursion, and it CANNOT be used: taken alone it faults
 (probe-closure-base-show, and probe-closure-basecase before it), while `even-fn 4`
 four levels deeper answers. That inversion is real and unexplained --
 PR 79 says so in as many words -- and a tier is not the place to argue
 it.

 `under-mutual` is the defect. `even-fn 4` recurses through `odd-fn` and
 returns `add3 5`; the two remaining arguments then arrive separately.
 The bare-metal answer is a pointer printed as an integer, so the row
 reads `not-47` there and `47` on an arm that keeps the arity. It is
 admitted in gold/EXPECTED.txt, and it is the whole point of the file
 that the admission becomes STALE the day the arms agree.

 The two spaces before each value are load-bearing: tier_run's line_key
 splits a row on the first double space, so a single space would key the
 ledger on `under-mutual not-47` -- value included -- and the admission
 would stop matching the moment the value it names changes, which is the
 one moment it has to speak.

 The ZIG arm could not build this file at all until 2026-08-24, which
 was finding 40: it emitted `even_fn(4, 20, 22)` against a definition it
 emitted as `fn even_fn(n: i64) CxFn2(i64, i64, i64)`. `control-pick`
 survived there only because the pipeline inlines `pick` and
 materialises the closure at the call site; `even-fn` is mutually
 recursive and nothing can inline it. The tier was EXCLUDED from the set
 for exactly as long as that lasted.

 Finding 40 is FIXED (`zig-plug-curried-apply`) and the exclusion is
 gone, which is what it was there to allow. The plug now emits
 `b5: { const _o5 = even_fn(4); break :b5 _o5.call(_o5.ctx, 20, 22); }`
 -- flat at the emitted arity, then the remainder through the closure --
 and this file compiles, runs, and answers `47` on the row where bare
 metal answers `not-47`.

 So the file IS the detector now. The two controls agree across the
 arms; `under-mutual` does not, and that single disagreement is
 COMPILER-18 with nothing else mixed into it. It is admitted in
 gold/EXPECTED.txt, tier_run marks it `ex`, and the day bare metal
 keeps the arity the arms will AGREE and the mark becomes `??` -- which
 is the ledger saying, without anyone remembering to check, that either
 the finding closed or the admission went stale.

 Expected on bare metal:

   control-flat  47
   control-pick  47
   under-mutual  not-47

Section: Shapes

 Full arity, no closure. The floor.

  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (x) (y) (z) = x + y + z

 Two return paths, no recursion. The control for everything the defect
 row also does: a closure-returning annotation, and three arguments
 arriving at a partial application.

  pick : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  pick (n) = if n == 0 then add3 5 else add3 6

 Mutual recursion returning a partial application. Neither definition
 calls itself, so no self-call test sees anything here; the arity is
 lost in the representation, not in a loop transformation.

  even-fn : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  even-fn (n) = if n == 0 then add3 5 else odd-fn (n - 1)

  odd-fn : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  odd-fn (n) = even-fn n

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("control-flat  " & show (add3 5 20 22))
    print-line-uni ("control-pick  " & (if (((pick 0) 20) 22) == 47 then "47" else "not-47"))
    print-line-uni ("under-mutual  " & (if (((even-fn 4) 20) 22) == 47 then "47" else "not-47"))
  end
